2513 Section 15. Section 20A-7-602.5 is amended to read:
2514 20A-7-602.5. Initial fiscal and legal impact statement -- Preparation of statement.
2517 (1) Within three business days after the day on which the local clerk receives a referendum
application, the local clerk shall submit a copy of the referendum application to the [county, city, or
town's] county's, city's, town's, or school district's budget officer.
2520 (2)
(a) The budget officer, together with legal counsel, shall prepare an unbiased, good faith initial fiscal
and legal impact statement for repealing the law the referendum proposes to repeal that contains:
2523 (i) a dollar amount representing the total estimated fiscal impact of repealing the law;
2524 (ii) if repealing the law would increase or decrease taxes, a dollar amount representing the total
estimated increase or decrease for each type of tax that would be impacted by the law's repeal
- 78 -

HB0170 compared with HB0170S01
and a dollar amount representing the total estimated increase or decrease in taxes that would
result from the law's repeal;
2528 (iii) if repealing the law would result in the issuance or a change in the status of bonds, notes, or
other debt instruments, a dollar amount representing the total estimated increase or decrease in
public debt that would result;
2531 (iv) a listing of all sources of funding for the estimated costs that would be associated with the
law's repeal, showing each source of funding and the percentage of total funding that would be
provided from each source;
2534 (v) a dollar amount representing the estimated costs or savings, if any, to state and local
government entities if the law were repealed;
2536 (vi) the legal impacts that would result from repealing the law, including:
2537 (A) any significant effects on a person's vested property rights;
2538 (B) any significant effects on other laws or ordinances;
2539 (C) any significant legal liability the city, county, or town may incur; and
2540 (D) any other significant legal impact as determined by the budget officer and the legal counsel; and
2542 (vii) a concise explanation, not exceeding 100 words, of the information described in this
Subsection (2)(a) and of the estimated fiscal impact, if any, if the law were repealed.
2545 (b)
(i) If repealing the law would have no fiscal impact, the local budget officer shall include a summary
statement in the initial fiscal impact and legal statement in substantially the following form:
2548 "The (title of the local budget officer) estimates that repealing the law this referendum proposes
to repeal would have no significant fiscal impact and would not result in either an increase or
decrease in taxes or debt."
2551 (ii) If repealing the law is estimated to have a fiscal impact, the local budget officer shall include a
summary statement in the initial fiscal and legal impact statement describing the fiscal impact.
2554 (iii) If the estimated fiscal impact of repealing the law is highly variable or is otherwise difficult to
reasonably express in a summary statement, the local budget officer may include in the summary
statement a brief explanation that identifies those factors impacting the variability or difficulty of the
estimate.
2558 (3) Within 20 calendar days after the day on which the local clerk submits a copy of the application
under Subsection (1), the budget officer shall:
- 79 -

HB0170 compared with HB0170S01
2560 (a) send a copy of the initial fiscal impact and legal statement to the local clerk's office; and
2562 (b) send a copy of the initial fiscal impact and legal statement to the first three sponsors named in the
referendum application.
2562 Section 16. Section 20A-7-602.7 is amended to read:
2563 20A-7-602.7. Referability to voters of local law other than land use law.
2566 (1) Within 20 calendar days after the day on which an eligible voter files a referendum application
under Section 20A-7-602 for a local school tax law, or a local law other than a land use law, counsel
for the county, city, [or town] town, or school district to which the referendum [pertains] relates
shall:
2570 (a) review the referendum application to determine whether the proposed referendum is legally
referable to voters; and
2572 (b) notify the first three sponsors, in writing, whether the proposed referendum is:
2573 (i) legally referable to voters; or
2574 (ii) rejected as not legally referable to voters.
2575 (2) For a local school tax law, or a local law other than a land use law, a proposed referendum is legally
referable to voters unless:
2577 (a) the proposed referendum challenges an action that is administrative, rather than legislative, in
nature;
2579 (b) the proposed referendum challenges more than one law passed by the local legislative body or the
local school board; or
2581 (c) the referendum application was not timely filed or does not comply with the requirements of this
part.
2583 (3) After the end of the 20-calendar-day period described in Subsection (1), [a county, city, or town
may not, for a local law other than a land use law] a county, city, town, or school district may not,
for a local school tax law, or a local law other than a land use law:
2586 (a) reject a proposed referendum as not legally referable to voters; or
2587 (b) except as provided in Subsection (4), challenge, in a legal action or otherwise, a proposed
referendum on the grounds that the proposed referendum is not legally referable to voters.
2590 (4)
(a) If, under Subsection (1)(b)(ii), a county, city, [or town] town, or school district rejects a proposed
referendum concerning a local school tax law, or a local law other than a land use law, a sponsor
